					"role": "assistant",
					"content": "`context.Context` in Go carries request-scoped information across function and goroutine boundaries. Its main purposes are coordinating cancellation, enforcing deadlines or timeouts, and storing small, immutable values associated with a request—such as trace IDs or authentication metadata.\n\nA function that may perform work, block, or make I/O should generally accept a context as its first parameter:\n\n```go\nfunc FetchUser(ctx context.Context, id string) (User, error)\n```\n\nCallers can derive contexts with `context.WithCancel`, `context.WithTimeout`, or `context.WithDeadline`, then call the returned cancellation function when work should stop. Functions should monitor `ctx.Done()` or check `ctx.Err()` and return promptly when cancellation occurs.\n\nContexts are commonly passed from HTTP or RPC handlers down through database, network, and service layers. They should not be stored in structs, used as a general-purpose parameter bag, or passed as `nil`; use `context.Background()` or `context.TODO()` when no better context is available.",
